  const CATEGORIES = [{"key": "mikrolimano", "label": "Mikrolimano — the named houses", "lead": "The three named houses on the crescent: the chef who cooked at sea, the family fish house of 1932, the plainest grill on the quay.", "story": {"title": "Three houses worth the touts", "story": "The crescent of Mikrolimano was the ancient Munichia, the smallest of the three war-harbours, and since about 1960 it has been the port's showroom for fish. Most of its terraces live off the view. Three houses earn the walk past the hosts. Lefteris Lazarou, a ship-cook's son who first cooked at sea at fifteen, won Greek cooking its first MICHELIN star in 2002 and brought his Seaside room down to this quay in 2013. Dourabeis has salt-grilled fish below Kastella since 1932, a family from Ios and Kythira still at the door. Papaioannou sends the catch out with almost nothing done to it, the rule a father taught in a back-street fish shop.", "where": "Varoulko Seaside and Papaioannou on Akti Koumoundourou; Dourabeis on Akti Dilaveri"}}, {"key": "fry-hill", "label": "The fry and the hill", "lead": "A fry with no menu since 1944 by the Naval Academy, and a 1957 taverna at the top of Kastella with the gulf below.", "story": {"title": "No menu, and the highest table", "story": "Two old family rooms at opposite heights. Beside the Hellenic Naval Academy, Margaro has fried whatever the boats brought since 1944, when its founder came over from Mykonos: there is no card, a grandchild recites the four or five things on offer, and the retsina is the family's own from Markopoulo vines. Up on the Profitis Ilias hill, a docker from Crete turned a 1930s kafeneio by the chapel into a taverna in 1957; his grandson works the pans, and from the terrace the Acropolis stands off one shoulder and Salamis and Aegina lie low on the water off the other. One is a queue; the other is a booking.", "where": "Margaro in Hatzikyriakio; Panorama on the Kastella hill"}}, {"key": "working-port", "label": "The working port — the auction & Keratsini", "lead": "The canteen inside the fish auction, and the ouzeri on Keratsini's little harbour under a dead power station.", "story": {"title": "Where the fish comes ashore", "story": "Nearly all of Attica's fish is auctioned at Keratsini before dawn, and two tables sit close enough to hear it. Inside the auction, a couple who came from Albania as newlyweds rebuilt an abandoned canteen in 2018 from tarps and pallets; he crosses to the opposite dock at three in the morning to choose the fish and fries it a few metres from your plate. Along the shore, on the little harbour under the shut power station and the container cranes, a couple have run an ouzeri for over thirty years on an older arrangement: the fishermen eat half of what they land and sell the rest to the kitchen.", "where": "Oinomageireio o Vasilis inside the Ichthyoskala; To Limanaki tou Giorgou on the Keratsini harbour"}}, {"key": "ouzeri", "label": "The ouzeri & the meze", "lead": "A captain's eight tables, a producer-built meze kitchen in a 1920 house, and the Ikarian sailors' kafeneio.", "story": {"title": "The drink first, the plates to slow it", "story": "At an ouzeri the order is inverted: the drink is the point and the small plates arrive to keep you at the table. Three rooms keep the rite their own way. A Kimolos sea captain filled a former nautical warehouse with anchors and liner relics and named it for the ships that carried Greek migrants to Australia: eight tables, and the ladenia of his island. In a 1920 neoclassical house in Freattyda a Santorini-trained cook builds the meze producer by producer. And off the port gates, the Ikarian diaspora's kafeneio, carried whole to a new street in 2023, sends out plates no one ordered with the tsipouro.", "where": "To Yperokeaneion in Hatzikyriakio; Dyosmos in Freattyda; Ikariotiko Kafeneio on Aristeidou"}}, {"key": "koutoukia", "label": "The barrel rooms & koutoukia", "lead": "Retsina from the barrel since 1920, the 1896 room where the quartet played, and a 1958 fig-tree yard in Kokkinia.", "story": {"title": "Barrels under the floor", "story": "Before bottled wine, a Piraeus wine-shop pressed its own and drew it from the cellar, and three rooms still work that way. In Agios Dionysios a family from Gardiki has drawn barrel retsina under the floor since 1920; in the Occupation the cellar was the quarter's air-raid shelter. A few streets off, shipwrights from Perama built a stone wine-house that first poured in 1896, and its cellar barrels still hold wine made on the spot. Up in Palaia Kokkinia, in the quarter the refugees built, a koutouki has cooked round a whitewashed fig tree since 1958. Order the wine by the karafa.", "where": "To Eidikon and Propolemikon in Agios Dionysios; To Koutouki tou Antoni in Palaia Kokkinia"}}, {"key": "refugee", "label": "The refugee kitchens — Kokkinia, Nikaia & Drapetsona", "lead": "The kitchens of 1922: Smyrna at a Nikaia table, and the oldest taverna in Drapetsona.", "story": {"title": "1922, behind the cranes", "story": "In 1922 and 1923 the refugees of Asia Minor and the Black Sea landed at this port and built new quarters on its edge: Nea Kokkinia, now Nikaia, Drapetsona, Keratsini. Their kitchens are still cooking. In a 1938 building on Agiou Ioannou in Nikaia the handmade Smyrna soutzoukakia and the pastourma pie go first, to live strings at the weekend. Opposite the church of Agios Panteleimon in Drapetsona, a dairy shop of 1930 turned to the charcoal grill and became the quarter's oldest taverna; a 1930 refrigerator still runs, and about six pots change every day. Neither is a showpiece. Both are where the refugee city still eats.", "where": "Alargino in Nikaia; Taverna tou Katsogiannou in Drapetsona"}}, {"key": "other", "label": "The other kitchens", "lead": "Constantinople on the Mikrolimano quay, a six-table Thai room in the refugee grid, the shipping offices' sushi bar.", "story": {"title": "The port's other kitchens", "story": "A port takes in more than fish. On the Mikrolimano quay, three coves from the touts, a room has cooked the Politiki kitchen of Constantinople for more than twenty years: hunkar begendi, adana with yogurt, pastourma, and some nights the old city's songs. In Nikaia, in a grid of low refugee houses, a Thai cook pounds her own curry pastes for six tables. Behind the sailors' church of Agios Nikolaos, near the shipping offices of Akti Miaouli, a small sushi bar has spent about a decade as the canteen of the people who move ships. None of it is Aegean. All of it is the port.", "where": "Vosporos on Akti Koumoundourou; Toukta in Nikaia; Sushi Rolls on Agiou Nikolaou"}}, {"key": "souvlaki", "label": "The grill and the wrap", "lead": "The port's argument about meat in pita: the cult meatball wrap, the last Armenian charcoal, the Olympiacos shrine.", "story": {"title": "Meat, salt, fire", "story": "The port argues about meat in pita the way it argues about fish. In the old flea-market lanes behind the fish market, a father and son set up in 2019 to make it 'as it used to be', a hand-formed beef patty in their own pita, and drew a queue within a few years. In Kokkinia, an Armenian survivor of Smyrna who once sold nuts from a cart learned to grill kebab around 1972; his children keep the dogma of salt, pepper and charcoal. And near the Olympiacos ground, a standing-room grill hung floor to ceiling in red and white has wrapped for nearly fifty years.", "where": "O Giorgos on Gounari; Souvlakia o Serkos on Plateia Neraidas; O Karabelas on Pylis"}}, {"key": "breakfast", "label": "Breakfast & the dawn pies", "lead": "The custard pie since 1909, and the Pontic piroski fried from three in the morning.", "story": {"title": "The morning, the Pontic way and the dairy way", "story": "The port's morning has two pedigrees. A grandfather delivered milk round Profitis Ilias by donkey in 1909; four generations on, his family's galaktoboureko is the custard pie the whole port measures against, sold over the counter to take away. Near the ferry gates, a woman who fled Trebizond in 1922 carrying her family's piroski recipe opened her own shop in 1977; her son, a former captain, starts the dough at three in the morning, and the fried pockets of cheese and mince are gone by midday.", "where": "Mastronikolas on Karaoli & Dimitriou; Euxeinon on Navarchou Notara"}}, {"key": "late-night", "label": "Late night — after the boat", "lead": "The 24-hour phyllo workshop in Nikaia, and the canteen on the Lipasmata quay.", "story": {"title": "After the boat", "story": "A port that works all night eats all night, and its late canon is phyllo and pork. Deep in residential Nikaia, a workshop founded in 1974 keeps the ovens going round the clock, and To Vima wrote this March that it still does: sweet custard or feta, cut from the tray, a taxi ride from the harbour. On the Drapetsona quay, among trucks and the derelict conveyor of the fertiliser works, a canteen papered with photographs of General Plastiras grills pork skewers for the port's shifts; its hours are whatever the port needs, so check before you go.", "where": "Bougatsa Pavlidi on Petrou Ralli, Nikaia; Kantina o Plastiras at Gate E1/E2, Drapetsona"}}];
